<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artavasdes II of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 31 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artavasdes II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from about 55 BCE, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>. He balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, and was blamed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> for the failure of his Parthian campaign of 36 BCE. Antony captured him in 34 BCE and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was later executed on Cleopatra's orders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artavasdes-ii-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Artavasdes II of Armenia" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Museum of Fine Arts Boston · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artavasdes_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
